{"rulings":[{"amendment_id":"fr-5-52f42a9a","decision":"reject","rationale":"The balance of evidence shows this amendment is stale against the current tree, not a live defect. In the current code, [crates/tank_core/src/types/snapshot.rs] imports and uses `dominant_repro_suppression_label(state)` rather than maintaining its own five-factor snapshot-only calculation, and [crates/tank_core/src/systems/shrimp.rs] builds the shared reproduction factor list with `molt_stress` included alongside temperature, condition, stability, density, TAN, and nitrite. That shared helper is exactly what the snapshot uses, so molt stress is already represented. The dedicated regression test `test_snapshot_uses_runtime_suppression_labels` also passes and explicitly asserts that a high `molt_stress_index` yields `\"molt_stress\"` as the snapshot suppression label. Those concrete facts outweigh the amendment’s claim that snapshot diagnostics still omit `f_molt`."}],"summary":"Rejected the disputed amendment because the current implementation already routes snapshot suppression labeling through the shared runtime factor logic, includes molt stress in that factor set, and has a passing test that verifies `molt_stress` is reported as the dominant suppressor."}